var PEDHD_NEO_DRAFT_A = [

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-1', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  image:'q-pd-hd-54',
  imgAlt:'Grayscale frontal (AP) chest radiograph of a newborn infant.',
  stem:'Natasha, a female infant, is delivered by caesarean section at 32 weeks’ gestation because of maternal pre-eclampsia. Her birth weight is 1.9 kg. No resuscitation is required. At 2 hours of age she develops respiratory distress, with a respiratory rate of 70 breaths/min, grunting respirations, and indrawing of her rib cage. Respiratory support with CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure) and 45% oxygen is required. A chest X-ray is taken at 4 hours of age, and is shown in the opposite figure. What is the most likely reason that this baby needs oxygen therapy and respiratory support?',
  options:['Aspiration of meconium has resulted in lung collapse','Blood is still flowing from the pulmonary artery to the aorta as in the fetal circulation','The alveoli still contain fluid','The chest wall and ribs are too compliant','There is ventilation-perfusion mismatch from surfactant deficiency'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**This is respiratory distress syndrome, and the question is asking for its mechanism rather than its name. The course material states that mechanism in one sentence.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` prints: *“RDS is the result of pulmonary immaturity and surfactant deficiency”*, and then the consequence — *“These factors will result in poor pulmonary compliance, atelectasis, decreased gas exchange and severe hypoxia and acidosis”*. Alveoli that collapse on expiration are still perfused but no longer ventilated, and blood leaving them is unoxygenated: that is ventilation-perfusion mismatch. It is why 45% oxygen alone is not enough and why CPAP, which splints the alveoli open through expiration, is what she actually needs.\n\n**Why 32 weeks is the whole story.** The same deck gives the timetable — *“Surfactant synthesis begins at 24-28 weeks and is mature by 35 weeks of gestation”* — and the epidemiology — *“The incidence of RDS is inversely proportional to gestational age: 60-80% of <28wk GA, 15-30% of 32-36wk GA”*. Natasha sits inside that second band. Her risk-factor list is the deck’s own: *“Preterm infants”* and *“Infants born by caesarean section”* are items 01 and 04. The deck even opens with a near-identical vignette — a 30-week preterm delivered by emergency caesarean for maternal pre-eclampsia, tachypnoeic at 70/min with grunting and retractions.\n\n**The film.** The expected appearance is given exactly: *“small lung volume, a fine reticulogranular ground-glass mottling in both lung fields through which an air bronchogram is seen”* — bilateral and uniform, which is what separates it on sight from every other option here.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Aspiration of meconium has resulted in lung collapse | Meconium aspiration is a **term and post-term** problem. The deck files it under *“Term and post-term infants who suffered from placental insufficiency”* and requires meconium-stained liquor, which is not described here. Its film is *“bilateral scattered areas of atelectasis, obstructive emphysema and pneumonitis”* — patchy and over-inflated, not uniformly small-volume |\n| Blood is still flowing from the pulmonary artery to the aorta as in the fetal circulation | This describes a persistently patent ductus with a right-to-left shunt. The duct is genuinely a preterm problem — `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` says *“PDA is markedly more common in preterm infants than at term”* and puts ductal constriction at **12-48 h** — but at 2 hours of age the duct is still normally open in every baby, and a ductal shunt does not produce grunting and rib-cage indrawing. Those are alveolar signs |\n| The alveoli still contain fluid | This is transient tachypnoea, *“also known as RDS type 2 or wet lung… delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid by lymphatics”*, whose top risk factor is *“Elective CS”* — so it is a real contender on the delivery history alone. It fails on gestation and severity: TTN *“occurs in term and late preterm neonates”*, is *“mild and transient”*, and its film is *“perihilar streaking with fluid in interlobar fissures”*. A 32-weeker needing CPAP and 45% oxygen is past that |\n| The chest wall and ribs are too compliant | True of every preterm, and it is exactly why she is drawing in her rib cage rather than expanding it — so this option explains a **sign**, not the oxygen requirement. A compliant chest wall wastes the work of breathing; it does not by itself put unoxygenated blood into the systemic circulation. *(The chest-wall mechanics are implied but not spelled out in the cached lectures beyond “poor pulmonary compliance” — not taken from the course material.)* |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** the deck’s prevention line is antenatal steroids — *“Betamethasone or (Dexamethasone) given I.M. to the mother 48 hours before delivery of fetus at 34 weeks or less”* — and the treatment line is supportive care, surfactant and assisted ventilation. Watch for the two complications it names next: intraventricular haemorrhage, and air leak from the ventilation itself.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Attribute the oxygen requirement in preterm respiratory distress syndrome to ventilation-perfusion mismatch across surfactant-deficient, atelectatic alveoli, and separate it from meconium aspiration, a ductal shunt, retained fetal lung fluid and the compliant preterm chest wall.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.54 (Part I, ch.7 Q1)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-2', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal',
  stem:'Robert is a full term male infant, born 10 hours ago. His mother is blood group O rhesus positive and her membranes ruptured 2 days before delivery. He is breastfeeding well but the midwife noticed he looks jaundiced. On examination the baby is clinically well but markedly jaundiced. What investigation should be performed first?',
  options:['Bilirubin level','Blood culture','Blood group','Congenital infection screen','Direct antibody test'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Jaundice before 24 hours of age is never normal, but the first test is the one that decides whether the baby needs treating in the next hour. That is the bilirubin level.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` makes the measurement itself the pivot: *“Excess accumulation of bilirubin in blood depends on both the rate of bilirubin production and the rate of excretion. It is best determined by comparing an hour specific TSB level to a standard curve of TSB concentration by age in hours.”* Nothing else on this list can be plotted against age in hours, and nothing else tells you whether to start phototherapy. The deck sets the threshold to act as a level: *“Phototherapy is started electively when the TSB is approximately 6 mg/dL (102 mmol/L) lower than the predicted exchange level for that infant.”*\n\n**Why the timing makes it urgent rather than routine.** The same deck prints the rule that turns Robert from a well baby into a problem: *“Visible jaundice with a TSB greater than 5 mg/dL before 24 hours of age is most commonly a result of significant hemolysis”*, and physiologic jaundice is defined by *“Visible jaundice appearing after 24 hours of age”*. He is 10 hours old and markedly jaundiced, so haemolysis is the working assumption — and a mother who is group O is the classic setup, since *“15% of pregnancies are ‘setups’ for ABO incompatibility (mother O, infant A or B)”*.\n\n**The rest of the panel is not cancelled, only sequenced.** The deck’s EVALUATION slide lists *“Assessment of blood type, Coombs testing, complete blood count (CBC) with smear, serum albumin, and TSB”* together, and all of it will be sent. The bank is asking which one comes **first**, and the answer is the one that changes management immediately.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Blood culture | Membranes ruptured 2 days before delivery, and `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` does count *“prolonged (>18 hours) or prelabour rupture of the membranes”* as an early-onset sepsis risk factor — so this is the strongest distractor on the page. It fails on the examination: the deck’s septic newborn is *“Not doing well… Off colors, earthy look… Hypo/hyperthermia… poor feeding”*, and Robert is feeding well and clinically well. Culture the baby who looks septic, not the baby who looks yellow |\n| Blood group | It will be sent, and with a group O mother it is how ABO incompatibility is confirmed — but the group does not tell you whether to switch on the lights. It answers *why*, after the bilirubin has answered *how bad* |\n| Congenital infection screen | Wrong compartment and wrong tempo. Congenital infection produces **conjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia — *“Jaundice due to conjugated hyperbilirubinemia is seen in some congenital infections, intrahepatic cholestasis, or extrahepatic obstruction of the biliary tract”* — with hepatosplenomegaly and growth failure, and it is a screen for prolonged jaundice, not for day-one jaundice |\n| Direct antibody test | The Coombs test is the immune-haemolysis discriminator — *“Immune mediated: Positive direct antibody (DAT, Coombs) test”* covers ABO, Rh and minor group incompatibility. Same objection as blood group: it is part of the cause workup that runs alongside, not the measurement that triggers treatment |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** send the total serum bilirubin now, plot it on the hour-specific chart, and send group, Coombs, blood count and film with it. If the level is near the exchange threshold the deck wants intensive phototherapy started at once, because *“Intensive phototherapy should decrease TSB by 30%-40% in the first 24 hours.”*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In a well term baby jaundiced before 24 hours, measure the total serum bilirubin first and plot it against age in hours, because that level alone decides treatment, while blood group, Coombs test, culture and congenital infection screen answer cause rather than urgency.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.54 (Part I, ch.7 Q2)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-3', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal-sepsis',
  stem:'Isabelle was born at term weighing 4 kg. At 6 hours of age she was noted to be breathing fast and have a low temperature. She was born by normal vaginal delivery and the membranes had ruptured 24 hours previously. Isabelle has not breastfed since birth and has had one vomit. On examination, she is lethargic and her core temperature is 35.5°C. She has a respiratory rate of 90 breaths/min, her central capillary refill time is 4 seconds, pulse 180/min and oxygen saturation is 89% in air. Her chest X-ray shows consolidation at the right base. What is the most likely causative organism for her infection?',
  options:['Escherichia coli (E. coli)','Group B streptococcus','Herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection','Listeria monocytogenes','Staphylococcus aureus'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Sepsis at 6 hours after prolonged rupture of membranes is early-onset sepsis, and the course material names its organism twice, in two different decks.**\n\n**The direct statements.** `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` splits the disease at 72 hours and prints the organisms in the split itself: EONS is *“first 72 hours of life”*, *“Vertical or horizontal”* transmission, *“Asymptomatic at birth, pneumonia or septicemia”*, *“Maternal Risk factors”*, *“GBS and Echoli”*. `30) Pneumonia.pdf` narrows it further with an age table whose first row is *“0-48 hours — Group B Streptococci”*, and adds: *“Neonate younger than 3 weeks. Infection is through vertical transmission from the mother during birth. Group B Streptococci and Gram negative enterococci and bacilli are the most common bacterial pathogens.”* Isabelle is 6 hours old with lobar consolidation. Both decks point to the same organism.\n\n**Her history is the GBS risk list, item by item.** The sepsis deck prints the maternal risk factors for early-onset GBS as *“preterm, especially preterm prolonged rupture of the membranes”*, *“prolonged (>18 hours) or prelabour rupture of the membranes”*, *“intrapartum fever >38°C or chorioamnionitis”*, *“previous child with GBS infection”* and *“GBS bacteriuria during pregnancy”*. Membranes ruptured 24 hours before delivery — over the 18-hour line. The colonisation arithmetic explains why this is common enough to be the default answer: *“Around 15%-40% of pregnant women have rectal or vaginal colonization of group B streptococci; about 50% of these infants become colonized and 1%-2% of the colonized infants develop early-onset infection.”*\n\n**And she is genuinely septic, not merely tachypnoeic.** Her signs map onto the deck’s clinical picture almost line for line: *“Hypo/hyperthermia”* (35.5°C), *“Tachypnea /apnea/ RD”* (90/min, saturation 89%), *“Heart rate variability”* and *“Hypotension, shock”* (pulse 180, capillary refill 4 seconds), *“Hypoactivity”* (lethargic) and *“FI, vomiting, poor feeding”* (not fed, one vomit). The sepsis deck also warns that hypothermia is not incidental — *“Strong and mutual relation between hypothermia and sepsis… Hypothermia can cause suppression of immunity.”*\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Escherichia coli (E. coli) | The **second** organism in the same early-onset pair, so it is the honest runner-up rather than a wrong answer in kind. `30) Pneumonia.pdf` moves it into the 2-14 day band, and `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` ties the Gram-negative picture to the preterm and low-birth-weight infant. Isabelle is term and 4 kg, and she presented at 6 hours — the GBS window, not the coliform one |\n| Herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection | Not in the neonatal sepsis deck at all. `infectious diseases causing vesicular rash.pdf` is where herpes lives, and neonatal HSV characteristically presents later in the first fortnight with vesicles, seizures or a hepatitis picture. Lobar consolidation on a chest film at 6 hours is a bacterial pattern |\n| Listeria monocytogenes | The classic examination answer, and it is real — `18) febrile child and life threatening infections.pdf` lists *“Listeria monocytogenes”* alongside GBS and E. coli for the neonate-to-3-months meningitis band, and `30) Pneumonia.pdf` puts *“L.monocytogens”* in the 2-14 day pneumonia row. But it is rare, it is a food-borne maternal exposure the stem never mentions, and it is not one of the two organisms the sepsis deck names for early onset |\n| Staphylococcus aureus | The deck puts it on the other side of the 72-hour line: late-onset sepsis is *“after 72 hours of life”*, *“Horizontal transmission”*, and its organisms are *“CONS (staph epidermidis), staph. aureus”*. It is the catheter-and-ward organism, and Isabelle has been alive for 6 hours |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** the deck’s empirical regimen where local antibiogram data are unavailable is *“septicemia and pneumonia: ampicillin + genta/amikin”* — ampicillin is there for GBS and Listeria both, so the empirical choice does not turn on this question. Send the *“Triple tabs blood culture, CSF analysis and culture, urine analysis and culture”* first, and remember the deck’s warning that supportive care carries the first 24 hours because *“antibiotics needs at least 12 hours to work.”*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Date neonatal sepsis by the 72-hour line: presentation at 6 hours after membranes ruptured 24 hours earlier is early-onset, vertically acquired disease, whose organisms are group B streptococcus first and E. coli second, not the late-onset staphylococci.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.54 (Part I, ch.7 Q3; the question straddles the page break, with the rest of its stem, its options and its answer line printed at the top of p.55)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-4', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'gastroenterology',
  stem:'James was born at 39 weeks’ gestation by elective caesarean section because of pre-eclampsia. His birth weight was 3.7 kg. His mother breastfed him as soon as she had recovered from the general anaesthetic. He fed well but is vomiting after every feed. He is now 18 hours old and after the last 2 feeds he ‘vomited everything up’ and it was greenish in colour. On examination his temperature is 36.5°C and he is alert and hungry. His abdomen is not distended. He has not yet passed meconium. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Duodenal atresia','Hirschprung disease','Meconium ileus','Neonatal sepsis','Pyloric stenosis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Green vomit in a newborn is obstruction until proved otherwise, and a flat abdomen puts the obstruction high. High and complete, on day one, with no meconium passed, is duodenal atresia.**\n\n**The two rules that solve this question are printed side by side.** `22)Vomiting.pdf` gives them as consecutive bullets: *“Green bile in vomit is an emergency, as it suggests obstructed bowel”*; then *“In intestinal obstruction, the more proximal the obstruction, the more prominent the vomiting and the sooner it becomes bile-stained, unless the obstruction is proximal to the ampulla of Vater”*; then *“Abdominal distension in intestinal obstruction becomes increasingly pronounced the more distal the obstruction.”* James vomits after **every** feed, the vomit is bile-stained by 18 hours, and his abdomen is **not** distended. Each of those three findings pushes the lesion higher, and the summary slide repeats the alarm: *“If bile-stained, potential emergency — intestinal obstruction.”*\n\n**Bile-stained means below the ampulla.** Duodenal atresia is usually post-ampullary, which is why the vomit is green rather than clear, and why the obstruction is high enough that gas and succus never reach the colon — so no meconium is passed. He is alert and hungry because nothing systemic is wrong with him yet; the pathology is purely mechanical.\n\n**The association the deck does name.** `6) Genetically determined disease_.pdf` lists *“Duodenal atresia”* among the features of Down syndrome, next to *“Congenital heart defects (40%)”* and *“Hirschsprung disease.”* Both of the top two options on this list are Down associations, which is why they are printed together — the discriminator is not the syndrome, it is the level of the obstruction. *(Polyhydramnios on the antenatal scan and the “double bubble” of gas in stomach and proximal duodenum on the abdominal film are the two other classic signposts, and neither appears anywhere in the cached lectures — not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Hirschprung disease **(printed with one “s” and no possessive — the book’s spelling, preserved)** | The distal-obstruction answer, and the one the abdominal examination excludes. Aganglionosis begins at the rectum, so the bowel above it fills: the picture is progressive **abdominal distension** with delayed passage of meconium, typically beyond 48 hours, and vomiting that arrives late rather than after the first feeds. James is 18 hours old with a flat abdomen |\n| Meconium ileus | Distal again — inspissated meconium obstructing the terminal ileum, essentially always in cystic fibrosis — so the same distension objection applies, and it too presents over days rather than in the first 18 hours *(cystic fibrosis and meconium ileus are not covered in the cached peds lectures — not taken from the course material)* |\n| Neonatal sepsis | `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` does list *“FI, vomiting, poor feeding”* in the clinical picture, so vomiting alone will not exclude it — but the same slide expects *“Not doing well… Off colors, earthy look… Hypo/hyperthermia… Hypoactivity”*. James is normothermic at 36.5°C, alert and hungry. A septic newborn does not look hungry |\n| Pyloric stenosis | Excluded twice over. `22)Vomiting.pdf` prints *“Non-bilious vomiting, which increases in frequency and forcefulness over time, then becoming projectile”*, and the pylorus is proximal to the ampulla, so the vomit **cannot** be green. It also gives the wrong age: *“It presents at 2-8 weeks of age, irrespective of gestational age”*, and the summary slide repeats *“If projectile at 2-8 weeks of age, exclude pyloric stenosis”* |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** stop enteral feeds, pass a nasogastric tube and put up fluids, then get an abdominal radiograph and a surgical opinion the same day. The elective caesarean and general anaesthetic in the history are there to tempt you toward a benign feeding explanation; the colour of the vomit overrides all of it.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Read bile-stained neonatal vomiting as intestinal obstruction and localise it by the abdomen: prominent early vomiting with a flat abdomen and no meconium is a high, post-ampullary lesion such as duodenal atresia, while distension marks the distal obstructions and non-bilious projectile vomiting at 2-8 weeks marks pyloric stenosis.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.55 (Part I, ch.7 Q4)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-5', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  image:'q-pd-hd-55',
  imgAlt:'Grayscale frontal (AP) chest radiograph of a newborn infant.',
  stem:'Mohammed was born at term, weighing 3 kg. He is 6 hours old and on the postnatal ward with his mother, who has asked the midwife to review him as he is breathing very quickly. Mohammed did not breathe well immediately after birth and needed mask ventilation to establish breathing. By 5 minutes of age he was crying and was wrapped and handed to his mother. On examination he is breathing at 64 breaths/min, and there is mild chest recession. Breath sounds on the left side are reduced compared with the right. His oxygen saturation is 95%. The chest X-ray is shown in the opposite figure. What is the most likely diagnosis of this patient?',
  options:['Pneumonia','Pneumothorax','Respiratory distress syndrome','Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**One-sided reduced breath sounds after positive-pressure ventilation is an air leak. The stem gives you the cause, the sign and the side.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` describes it in five lines that this vignette satisfies one at a time: *“Pneumothorax may occur spontaneously. Most commonly, when on mechanical ventilation… unhealthy lung (RDS). Sudden deterioration of respiratory status. Increased oxygen requirements. Decreased breath sounds and chest movement in affected side. Chest bulge in affected side.”* Mohammed had **mask ventilation** to establish breathing, and now has decreased breath sounds on the left. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` adds the pathology and the second physical sign: air leaks arise when *“Over distension of alveolar sacs or terminal airways leads to disruption of airway integrity, resulting in dissection of air into surrounding spaces”*, producing *“1. Sudden RD and deterioration of clinical course… 2. Asymmetry of thorax is present in unilateral cases.”*\n\n**Asymmetry is the whole discriminator.** Every other diagnosis on this menu is bilateral or diffuse. A unilateral finding in a term baby who was bagged at birth has one common answer.\n\n**The film.** On the radiograph the affected hemithorax is hyperlucent with no lung markings out to the chest wall, the lung edge is visible, and if the leak is under tension the mediastinum is pushed away and the hemidiaphragm flattened. The deck names the bedside shortcut too — *“Diagnosis: Transillumination test CXR”* — because a cold light held against the chest wall lights up a pneumothorax faster than a radiograph can be taken. Treatment, where it is needed, is *“Chest tube with underwater seal”*; a small leak in a baby saturating 95% in air is often watched.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Pneumonia | Congenital pneumonia is *“Often indistinguishable from HMD +/- septicemia signs”* and arises from *“aspiration of infected amniotic fluid”* — so it needs a maternal risk factor, and it brings the sepsis picture with it. There is no prolonged rupture, no maternal fever, no temperature instability here, and Mohammed is saturating 95% in air. Consolidation is also usually a focal opacity, not a unilateral reduction in air entry after bagging |\n| Respiratory distress syndrome | Wrong gestation and wrong film. RDS is *“a leading cause of mortality and pulmonary morbidity in preterm infants”* with an incidence *“inversely proportional to gestational age”*, and its radiograph is *“a fine reticulogranular ground-glass mottling in both lung fields”* — **both**, uniformly. A term 3 kg baby with a one-sided finding is not this |\n| Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn | The right diagnosis for a baby who is profoundly and stubbornly hypoxaemic, and the deck reaches it through meconium aspiration, where it is listed as a consequence alongside chemical pneumonitis and airway obstruction. Saturation 95% in room air with only mild recession is far too well; PPHN does not present with a normal saturation and asymmetric breath sounds |\n| Bronchopulmonary dysplasia | Impossible at 6 hours. `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` defines chronic lung disease as *“Need for oxygen >36 weeks postmenstrual age”* — a diagnosis made after weeks of oxygen dependence in a preterm, not on the first morning of life in a term baby |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** whenever a newborn who received positive-pressure ventilation deteriorates or shows one-sided signs, think air leak before anything else, transilluminate immediately, and remember the deck’s reminder that a pneumothorax is also *“usually the complication of mechanical ventilation”* in babies already being treated for RDS.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Suspect pneumothorax when a newborn who received positive-pressure ventilation develops unilateral reduction in breath sounds and thoracic asymmetry, and use that asymmetry to exclude the bilateral or diffuse alternatives — respiratory distress syndrome, congenital pneumonia, persistent pulmonary hypertension and chronic lung disease.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.55 (Part I, ch.7 Q5)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-6', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  image:'q-pd-hd-56a',
  imgAlt:'Grayscale frontal (AP) chest radiograph of a newborn infant.',
  stem:'Sabrina was born at 37 weeks’ gestation, birth weight 2.8 kg. She was noted by the midwife to be breathing very fast at 2 hours of age. On examination she is breathing at 72 breaths/min and has moderate chest recession. The heart sounds are difficult to hear on the left and her apex beat is palpable on the right side of the chest. A chest X-ray is taken (shown in the opposite figure). What is the most likely diagnosis of this patient?',
  options:['Pneumonia','Pneumothorax','Respiratory distress syndrome','Diaphragmatic hernia','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Heart sounds lost on the left with the apex beat felt on the right means the mediastinum has been pushed across. In a newborn with respiratory distress, the course material attaches that sign to one diagnosis by name.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` gives congenital diaphragmatic hernia a slide of its own, and it is a three-item list: *“1. Severe, immediate respiratory distress, 2. Scaphoid (sunken) abdomen, 3. Heart sounds shifted to the right.”* Sabrina has the first and the third printed almost word for word. CDH is also the deck’s single named **extra-pulmonary** cause of neonatal respiratory distress, set apart from the pulmonary causes in its own branch of the causes diagram — which is the structural reason it is the odd one out on this option menu.\n\n**What the film shows.** A left-sided hernia lets bowel migrate through the defect into the chest, so the left hemithorax fills with gas-filled loops rather than lung, the left lung is compressed, the mediastinum and cardiac shadow are displaced to the right, and there is less gas than expected in the abdomen. A nasogastric tube whose tip comes to rest above the diaphragm on the film is confirmatory. The deck adds the timing: *“It is often diagnosed via prenatal ultrasound (18-20 weeks)”* — antenatal detection is the usual route, and this baby has slipped through it.\n\n**Why the sign matters more than the tachypnoea.** Every option on this menu can make a newborn breathe at 72/min. Only two can move the heart, and the stem hands you the one clue that separates them.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Pneumothorax | **The real competitor, and the reason to look at the film rather than the examination.** A tension pneumothorax on the left produces exactly the same mediastinal shift to the right, and `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` explicitly expects *“Asymmetry of thorax… in unilateral cases”*. Two things separate them: the history — `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` puts pneumothorax *“Most commonly, when on mechanical ventilation… unhealthy lung (RDS)”* and describes *“Sudden deterioration”*, whereas Sabrina was never ventilated and has been tachypnoeic steadily since 2 hours — and the radiograph, where a pneumothorax gives a uniformly black, structureless hemithorax while a hernia gives loops with walls inside it |\n| Pneumonia | Congenital pneumonia is *“Often indistinguishable from HMD”* and follows *“aspiration of infected amniotic fluid”*, so it wants a maternal risk factor the stem does not supply, and it produces consolidation rather than mediastinal displacement. Consolidation pulls nothing across |\n| Respiratory distress syndrome | Possible at 37 weeks but not likely — the incidence is *“inversely proportional to gestational age”*, and the deck brackets 32-36 weeks at 15-30%, with a late preterm sitting at the low end of that. Its film is *“small lung volume”* with bilateral ground-glass mottling and air bronchograms, which is a symmetrical picture with no shift |\n| Bronchopulmonary dysplasia | Ruled out by the clock. Chronic lung disease means *“Need for oxygen >36 weeks postmenstrual age”* after a prolonged preterm course; Sabrina is 2 hours old |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** if the diagnosis is entertained, do **not** give mask ventilation — bagging inflates the herniated bowel inside the chest and worsens the compression. Pass a nasogastric tube on free drainage, intubate if ventilation is needed, and refer to paediatric surgery. Look for the scaphoid abdomen the deck names; it is the sign that is easiest to elicit and easiest to forget.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognise congenital diaphragmatic hernia from respiratory distress with the heart sounds shifted away from the affected side, and separate it from a tension pneumothorax — which shifts the mediastinum identically — by the absence of positive-pressure ventilation and by bowel loops rather than a structureless hyperlucency on the film.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.56 (Part I, ch.7 Q6)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-7', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'perinatal-rd',
  image:'q-pd-hd-56b',
  imgAlt:'Grayscale frontal (AP) chest radiograph of an infant.',
  stem:'Thomas, a baby boy with a birth weight of 875 g at 28 weeks’ gestation required artificial ventilation for 2 weeks. At 10 weeks of age he still needs additional oxygen via nasal cannulae. This is his chest X-ray (shown in the opposite figure). What is the most likely diagnosis of this patient?',
  options:['Pneumonia','Pneumothorax','Respiratory distress syndrome','Diaphragmatic hernia','Bronchopulmonary dysplasia'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Bronchopulmonary dysplasia is defined by a date, not by a sign, and the arithmetic in this stem is the definition.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` gives chronic lung disease two lines: *“Need for oxygen >36 weeks postmenstrual age”* and *“Oxygen toxicity”*, and lists *“CLD”* fourth among the five respiratory problems of the preterm infant, after RDS type I, congenital pneumonia and pneumothorax and before apnoea of prematurity. `9) Respiratory distress in newborn.pdf` prints the name in full — *“Bronchopulmonary dysplasia”* — in its pulmonary-causes diagram. The two decks are describing the same disease under two names, which is worth knowing because this bank uses the second one.\n\n**Do the arithmetic.** Born at 28 weeks, now 10 weeks old: 28 + 10 = **38 weeks postmenstrual age**, and he is still in oxygen. That crosses the deck’s 36-week line, so the diagnosis is made by definition rather than by inference. Everything else in the stem is the standard route to it: extreme prematurity, an extremely low birth weight of 875 g — below the deck’s *“ELBW - < 1000 grams”* band — and two weeks of artificial ventilation.\n\n**How it comes about.** The deck attributes it to *“Oxygen toxicity”*, and the mechanism it implies is the combination the neonatal literature calls volutrauma plus hyperoxia: positive-pressure ventilation and supplemental oxygen delivered to a lung that stopped developing at the saccular stage, producing inflammation, arrested alveolarisation and abnormal pulmonary vasculature. *(The developmental-arrest account of “new” BPD is not spelled out in the cached lectures — not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**The film.** After weeks of ventilation the radiograph loses the uniform ground glass of acute RDS and becomes coarse and inhomogeneous — areas of hyperinflation and cystic lucency alternating with strands of fibrosis and collapse, with generally increased lung volume. The indwelling line visible on this radiograph belongs to the long neonatal-unit course, not to the diagnosis.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Pneumonia | An acute illness with fever or temperature instability, a new focal opacity and a septic picture — `7) Intro to neonatology.pdf` describes congenital pneumonia as arising from *“aspiration of infected amniotic fluid”* with *“Same organisms as in neonatal sepsis”*. A pneumonia can certainly complicate Thomas, but it does not explain a steady ten-week oxygen requirement |\n| Pneumothorax | The deck defines it by abruptness — *“Sudden deterioration of respiratory status. Increased oxygen requirements. Decreased breath sounds and chest movement in affected side”* — and it is treated with a chest drain and resolves. It is a plausible **event** during his two ventilated weeks; it is not a diagnosis that persists to 10 weeks on nasal cannulae |\n| Respiratory distress syndrome | This was almost certainly his illness on day one, and at 28 weeks the deck puts the incidence at *“60-80% of <28wk GA”* — but RDS is self-limiting: *“The condition then stabilizes and finally improves over the next few days with proper management.”* Persisting oxygen dependence at 10 weeks is the sequel, not the original disease. This distinction — acute RDS versus the chronic lung disease that follows it — is what the question is testing |\n| Diaphragmatic hernia | Congenital and immediate: *“Severe, immediate respiratory distress, Scaphoid (sunken) abdomen, Heart sounds shifted to the right”*, and *“often diagnosed via prenatal ultrasound (18-20 weeks)”*. It cannot appear for the first time at 10 weeks of age in a baby who was ventilated from birth |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** management is time and support — wean oxygen against saturation targets rather than a calendar, protect growth and nutrition because these babies burn calories breathing, immunise on schedule, and expect increased susceptibility to respiratory infection. `32)Acute bronchiloitis_.pdf` lists *“Chronic lung disease”* among the risk factors for severe bronchiolitis, which is the practical consequence of the diagnosis for his first winter.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Diagnose bronchopulmonary dysplasia — the chronic lung disease of prematurity — from continuing oxygen dependence beyond 36 weeks postmenstrual age after ventilation of an extremely low birth weight preterm, and distinguish it from the acute, self-limiting respiratory distress syndrome that preceded it.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.56 (Part I, ch.7 Q7)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-8', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal',
  stem:'Stewart is a full-term baby boy, born 16 hours ago. His mother is blood group O rhesus positive. The baby is breastfeeding well but the midwife has noticed he looks jaundiced. On examination the baby is clinically well. His bilirubin was 150 µmol/L at 10 hours and he was started on intensive phototherapy. Six hours later his bilirubin is 250 µmol/L. Stewart’s blood group was identified as group A rhesus positive. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the jaundice in that patient?',
  options:['ABO incompatibility','Biliary atresia','Breastmilk jaundice','Physiological jaundice','Rhesus hemolytic disease'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Group O mother, group A baby, jaundice inside 24 hours, bilirubin still climbing through intensive phototherapy. The course material calls that combination a setup by name.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf`: *“This finding can accompany any pregnancy in a type O mother. Hemolysis is usually mild, but the severity is unpredictable because of variability in the amount of naturally occurring maternal anti-A or anti-B IgG antibodies. Although 15% of pregnancies are ‘setups’ for ABO incompatibility (mother O, infant A or B), only 33% of infants in such cases have a positive direct Coombs test and less than 10% of these infants develop jaundice that requires therapy.”* Stewart is the mother-O, infant-A pairing exactly, and he is in the small fraction that needs treating.\n\n**The timing proves haemolysis before the blood groups are back.** The same deck prints the rule: *“Visible jaundice with a TSB greater than 5 mg/dL before 24 hours of age is most commonly a result of significant hemolysis”*, and puts ABO incompatibility at the head of its list of *“Immune mediated: Positive direct antibody (DAT, Coombs) test”* causes, alongside Rh and minor blood group incompatibility.\n\n**The rate of rise is the other half of the evidence.** From 150 µmol/L at 10 hours to 250 µmol/L at 16 hours is roughly 17 µmol/L per hour, and it happened **while intensive phototherapy was running** — the deck expects intensive phototherapy to *“decrease TSB by 30%-40% in the first 24 hours.”* A bilirubin that rises against treatment is being produced faster than it can be cleared, which is the definition of ongoing haemolysis. For scale, the deck caps physiologic jaundice at a rise of *“< 5 mg/dL (86 mmol/L) per day.”*\n\n**Note the book’s own spelling.** Option E is printed *“Rhesus hemolytic disease”*, without the diphthong, in a book that prints *“Group B haemolytic streptococcus”* with it at Q18. The bank contradicts itself on this word; both spellings are preserved as printed and neither has been tidied.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| Biliary atresia | Wrong bilirubin fraction and wrong week. It is a **conjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia — `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` requires jaundice *“beyond 2 weeks”*, *“pathognomonic pale (clay-colored) stools with dark urine”* and hepatomegaly, and states that *“Any infant who has jaundice after age 2 weeks should be evaluated for cholestasis.”* Stewart is 16 hours old and clinically well |\n| Breastmilk jaundice | Wrong tempo entirely. The deck describes *“Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia lasting until 2-3 months of age”* and *“Moderate unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia for 6-12 weeks in a thriving breastfed infant without evidence of hemolysis.”* It is a diagnosis of the second week onward, in a baby who is **not** haemolysing, and it never demands intensive phototherapy on day one |\n| Physiological jaundice | Excluded by the clock and by the slope. Its definition opens *“Visible jaundice appearing after 24 hours of age”*, with *“Peak bilirubin… at 3-5 days of age”* and a rise under 86 µmol/L per day. Stewart was jaundiced at 10 hours and rose 100 µmol/L in six |\n| Rhesus hemolytic disease **(printed without the diphthong — the book’s spelling, preserved)** | Serologically impossible here: **the mother is rhesus positive**, so she cannot be sensitised to the D antigen and produces no anti-D. The deck also gives it a different clinical shape — *“less common, more severe, and more predictable than ABO incompatibility”*, with affected neonates *“often anemic at birth”* and, at worst, the hydrops of erythroblastosis fetalis |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** send a direct antibody test, a blood count with film and a reticulocyte count, keep the intensive phototherapy running and re-check the level frequently against the exchange threshold. Then remember the long tail the deck warns about: *“Since maternal antibodies may persist for several months after birth, the newborn may become progressively more anemic over the first few weeks of life, occasionally to the point of requiring transfusion.”* Stewart needs a haemoglobin check after discharge, not just a bilirubin.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Diagnose ABO haemolytic disease from a group O mother with a group A or B infant jaundiced within 24 hours, using a bilirubin that rises against intensive phototherapy as the marker of ongoing haemolysis, and exclude rhesus disease outright when the mother is rhesus positive.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.56 (Part I, ch.7 Q8; the question straddles the page break, with its answer line printed alone at the top of p.57)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-9', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal',
  stem:'Alfie is a 3-week-old male infant whose mother is concerned that his stools are pale. He is breastfed. On examination he is jaundiced, has mild hepatomegaly and has only just regained his birthweight. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the jaundice in that patient?',
  options:['ABO incompatibility','Biliary atresia','Breastmilk jaundice','Physiological jaundice','Rhesus hemolytic disease'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Pale stools are the finding that ends the discussion. In a jaundiced infant past two weeks, they mean bile is not reaching the gut, and the commonest reason for that is biliary atresia.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` defines cholestasis as *“Failure of the bile to reach the intestine; due to impairment in bile formation and/or excretion”*, and then prints the clinical triad this stem reproduces: *“Neonatal cholestasis presents with jaundice (beyond 2 weeks), hepatomegaly, and pathognomonic pale (clay-colored) stools with dark urine (bilirubinuria).”* It names *“Failure to Thrive: Poor weight gain and growth”* separately — Alfie has only just regained his birth weight at three weeks — and gives the epidemiology outright: *“The most common cause of cholestatic jaundice in the first months of life is biliary atresia (up to 60%).”*\n\n**The rule that catches it.** The same deck prints it twice, in the etiology section and again in the key considerations: *“Any infant who has jaundice after age 2 weeks should be evaluated for cholestasis including with total and direct bilirubin levels.”* Alfie is three weeks old. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` says the same thing from the other side, listing a *“Fractionated bilirubin level in infants who appear ill, those with prolonged jaundice, acholic stool, hepatosplenomegaly, or dark urine to evaluate for cholestasis.”*\n\n**Why this is a surgical emergency dressed as a well-baby review.** The deck is emphatic about the clock: *“The diagnostic approach to neonatal cholestasis focuses on identifying biliary atresia (BA) immediately, ideally within the first 6 weeks of life, to enable timely surgical intervention”*; *“Liver Biopsy Timing: Highly recommended before 6 weeks of age”*; *“Timely Action: Early Kasai portoenterostomy (KPE) is critical for BA survival before 6 weeks of age.”* At three weeks Alfie still has time; at ten weeks he would not.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| ABO incompatibility | An **unconjugated** haemolytic jaundice of the first days, and haemolysis cannot make stools pale — bile pigment delivery to the gut is if anything increased. `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf` also requires a group O mother with a group A or B infant, which the stem does not offer, and hepatomegaly is not part of its picture |\n| Breastmilk jaundice | **The trap, because Alfie is breastfed and three weeks old.** It fails on the two hard findings. The deck defines it as *“Moderate unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia for 6-12 weeks in a **thriving** breastfed infant without evidence of hemolysis, hypothyroidism, or other disease”* — Alfie is not thriving, and his stools are pale. Unconjugated bilirubin never produces acholic stools |\n| Physiological jaundice | Ruled out by the calendar in its own definition: *“Visible jaundice resolves by 1 week in the fullterm infant and by 2 weeks in the preterm infant.”* Jaundice still present at three weeks is by definition prolonged, and prolonged jaundice is what triggers the cholestasis workup |\n| Rhesus hemolytic disease **(printed without the diphthong — the book’s spelling, preserved)** | Wrong week, wrong fraction, and no serology to support it. The deck describes affected neonates as *“often anemic at birth”* with *“continued hemolysis rapidly caus[ing] hyperbilirubinemia and worsening anemia”* in the first days, and its severe form is hydrops. Nothing about it makes stools pale |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** the deck’s sequence is *“Serum total/direct bilirubin, AST, ALT, Gamma-GT (GGT), alkaline phosphatase, and coagulation studies (PT/INR)”*, then abdominal ultrasound looking for a *“Small gall bladder, or the ‘triangular cord sign’”*, then percutaneous liver biopsy, *“Highly accurate (96.9%) for distinguishing obstructive jaundice from neonatal hepatitis”*, with operative cholangiography as *“the ‘gold standard’ for diagnosing biliary atresia.”* Give vitamin K while waiting: the deck warns that *“Deficiencies in vitamins A, D, E, and K can lead to coagulopathy.”*\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Treat pale stools with jaundice persisting beyond two weeks as conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and investigate for biliary atresia at once, using poor weight gain and hepatomegaly to separate it from the thriving breastfed infant of breastmilk jaundice, since Kasai portoenterostomy must be done before 6 weeks.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.57 (Part I, ch.7 Q9)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-10', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal',
  stem:'Poppy is 2 weeks old and is breastfed. She is thriving but is jaundiced. The midwife does a bilirubin which is moderately raised at 170 µmol/L, and is nearly all unconjugated. A urine dipstick is negative. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the jaundice in that patient?',
  options:['ABO incompatibility','Biliary atresia','Breastmilk jaundice','Physiological jaundice','Rhesus hemolytic disease'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Thriving is the word that decides this question, and the course material builds the diagnosis around it.**\n\n**The direct statement.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf`: *“B. BREAST-MILK JAUNDICE. Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia lasting until 2-3 months of age is common in breastfed infants. Moderate unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia for 6-12 weeks in a thriving breastfed infant without evidence of hemolysis, hypothyroidism, or other disease strongly suggests this diagnosis.”* Poppy is breastfed, thriving, two weeks old, moderately and almost entirely unconjugated at 170 µmol/L. Every clause of the definition is satisfied.\n\n**The distinction the deck insists on, and this question rewards.** It prints **two** breastfeeding jaundices, and they are opposites. *“A. BREAST-FEEDING-ASSOCIATED JAUNDICE. This common condition has also been called ‘lack of breastmilk’ jaundice… The pathogenesis is probably poor enteral intake and increased enterohepatic circulation. Excessive jaundice should be considered a possible sign of failure to establish an adequate milk supply. The best way to evaluate successful breastfeeding is to monitor the infant’s weight, urine, and stool output.”* That one is a **feeding failure** in a baby who is losing weight, and it is treated by fixing the feeding. Breast-milk jaundice is a benign persistence in a baby who is feeding perfectly well. Poppy is thriving, so she is the second.\n\n**Why the mechanism makes a well baby yellow.** The deck explains the newborn’s baseline handicap: *“Absence of gut flora and slow GI motility, both characteristics of the newborn, cause stasis of conjugated bilirubin in the intestinal lumen, where mucosal β-glucuronidase removes the glucuronide molecules and leaves unconjugated bilirubin to be reabsorbed (enterohepatic circulation).”* It also notes the constitutional contribution: infants with Gilbert syndrome *“are more likely to have prolonged neonatal jaundice and breastmilk jaundice.”*\n\n**The dipstick is not decoration.** A negative urine dipstick means no bilirubinuria — conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble and appears in urine, so its absence confirms what the fractionation already said, that this is an unconjugated jaundice and not cholestasis. It also argues against a urinary tract infection, which is a standard cause of prolonged neonatal jaundice: `11) Neonatal sepsis.pdf` lists *“Vomiting, fever, prolonged jaundice, poor weight gain”* as the non-specific presentation of neonatal UTI.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| ABO incompatibility | A haemolytic jaundice of the **first day** — the deck ties it to *“Visible jaundice with a TSB greater than 5 mg/dL before 24 hours of age”* and to a group O mother with a group A or B infant. Haemolysis at two weeks would show as anaemia and a raised reticulocyte count in a baby who was jaundiced from the start, not as an isolated moderate level in a thriving fortnight-old |\n| Biliary atresia | The dangerous one, and it is excluded by the numbers rather than by reassurance. `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` defines cholestasis as *“a conjugated bilirubin level (>1) mg/dL (or (>20%) of total serum bilirubin)”* with *“pathognomonic pale (clay-colored) stools with dark urine”* and failure to thrive. Poppy’s bilirubin is *“nearly all unconjugated”*, her dipstick is negative and she is thriving — the opposite of every one of those |\n| Physiological jaundice | Correct in kind, wrong in duration. Its own definition ends it: *“Visible jaundice resolves by 1 week in the fullterm infant and by 2 weeks in the preterm infant”*, with the peak at 3-5 days. Jaundice still clearly present at two weeks in a term baby has outlasted the label, which is exactly why a separate diagnosis exists |\n| Rhesus hemolytic disease **(printed without the diphthong — the book’s spelling, preserved)** | The deck calls it *“less common, more severe, and more predictable than ABO incompatibility”*, with neonates *“often anemic at birth”* and hyperbilirubinaemia developing rapidly from day one, frequently needing exchange transfusion. A thriving, well two-week-old with a moderate level is not this |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** breast-milk jaundice is a diagnosis of exclusion and the exclusions are cheap — confirm the split bilirubin, check the stools are pigmented and the urine clear, check weight gain, and send thyroid and urine if it drags on. Breastfeeding continues. The deck expects the jaundice to fade over 6-12 weeks; anything conjugated, or any pale stool, converts this into the biliary atresia pathway immediately.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify breastmilk jaundice as prolonged, moderate, unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia in a thriving breastfed infant, and separate it from breast-feeding-associated jaundice of poor intake, from physiological jaundice that should have resolved by one week, and from the conjugated jaundice of biliary atresia.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.57 (Part I, ch.7 Q10)'
},

{
  id:'pedhd-neo-11', bank:'house', module:'pediatrics', chapter:'neonatal',
  stem:'Dimitri is 20 hours old and is noted to be markedly jaundiced, needing intensive phototherapy. Maternal blood is group A rhesus positive. His blood group is group A rhesus positive. He has been breastfeeding well. On examination he is markedly jaundiced, but is alert and active. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the jaundice in that patient?',
  options:['ABO incompatibility','Biliary atresia','Breastmilk jaundice','G6PD deficiency','Rhesus hemolytic disease'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**This is the same menu as the three questions before it with one option swapped, and the swap is the question. Option D has changed from “Physiological jaundice” to “G6PD deficiency”, and the serology has been written to close off every immune cause.**\n\n**Start with what the timing forces.** `8) COMMON PROBLEMS IN THE TERM NEWBORN.pdf`: *“Visible jaundice with a TSB greater than 5 mg/dL before 24 hours of age is most commonly a result of significant hemolysis.”* Dimitri is 20 hours old, markedly jaundiced and already on intensive phototherapy. So he is haemolysing — and the deck sorts haemolysis into two boxes, *“Immune mediated: Positive direct antibody (DAT, Coombs) test”* and *“Nonimmune: negative direct antibody (DAT, Coombs) test.”*\n\n**Now close the immune box.** ABO incompatibility needs a group O mother — the deck states it flatly: *“This finding can accompany any pregnancy in a type O mother”*, and the setup is *“mother O, infant A or B.”* Dimitri’s mother is group **A** and so is he, so there is no maternal anti-A to cross. Rhesus disease needs a rhesus **negative** mother to be sensitised; his mother is rhesus positive. Both immune causes are excluded on the blood groups the stem deliberately supplies.\n\n**That leaves the non-immune box, and one of its two entries is on the option list.** The deck’s non-immune causes are *“Abnormal red cell shapes: spherocytosis, elliptocytosis, pyknocytosis, stomatocytosis”* and *“Red cell enzyme abnormalities: glucose-6-phosphate Dehydrogenase deficiency, pyruvate kinase deficiency, hexokinase deficiency.”* Only G6PD deficiency is offered, and it is the commonest of them: *“This condition is the most common red cell enzyme defect causing hemolysis, especially in infants of African, Mediterranean, or Asian descent.”* The deck makes it a specific test to send: *“G6PD test if jaundice is otherwise unexplained.”* Jaundice otherwise unexplained is precisely what this stem constructs.\n\n**The trap in confirming it.** *“Since G6PD enzyme activity is high in reticulocytes, neonates with a large number of reticulocytes may have falsely normal enzyme tests. A low G6PD level should always raise suspicion. Repeat testing in suspect cases with initially normal results is indicated at 3 months of age.”* A normal assay taken during the haemolytic episode does not exclude the diagnosis. The deck also notes it is not only a boys’ disease — *“female heterozygotes are also at increased risk of hyperbilirubinemia due to X-chromosome inactivation”* — and that the load is handled badly at both ends, since *“increased bilirubin production is further exaggerated by a decreased rate of bilirubin conjugation.”*\n\n**Why the other options are wrong**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| ABO incompatibility | Serologically impossible as written: the mother is group A, not group O, so she carries no naturally occurring anti-A IgG against a group A infant. This is the option the stem was built to exclude, and it is the one most readers pick from habit because the previous three questions rewarded it |\n| Biliary atresia | Wrong fraction and wrong month. `43)Infantile cholestasis_.pdf` requires **conjugated** hyperbilirubinaemia with jaundice *“beyond 2 weeks”*, pale stools, dark urine and hepatomegaly. Dimitri is 20 hours old, alert and active, and unconjugated jaundice does not respond to phototherapy if it is conjugated in the first place |\n| Breastmilk jaundice | Wrong tempo by an order of magnitude: *“Moderate unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia for 6-12 weeks in a thriving breastfed infant without evidence of hemolysis.”* It is mild, it belongs to the second week onward, and it never demands intensive phototherapy on day one. The stem mentions that he feeds well only to establish that intake is not the problem |\n| Rhesus hemolytic disease **(printed without the diphthong — the book’s spelling, preserved)** | Excluded by the mother being **rhesus positive**, so no anti-D can be raised. The deck also gives it a heavier presentation than this — *“Affected neonates are often anemic at birth”*, severity increasing with each immunised pregnancy, and at the extreme *“erythroblastosis fetalis… characterized by lifethreatening anemia, generalized edema, and fetal or neonatal heart failure”* |\n\n**The clinical bottom line:** confirm the negative direct antibody test, send a blood count, film and reticulocytes, send the G6PD assay knowing it may read falsely normal now and must be repeated at three months, and keep the intensive phototherapy going with the exchange threshold in view — the deck sets escalation at *“bilirubin levels within 2 mg/dL of the double volume exchange threshold”* or a failure to fall by *“at least 0.5 mg/dL/h after 4 hours.”* Then counsel the family about the oxidant triggers to avoid.\n\nWritten for this bank — pediatric .pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'When a newborn is jaundiced within 24 hours but the blood groups exclude both ABO and rhesus incompatibility, move from immune to non-immune haemolysis and name G6PD deficiency, the commonest red cell enzyme defect — remembering that a high reticulocyte count can make the assay falsely normal.',
  source:'pediatric .pdf p.57 (Part I, ch.7 Q11)'
},

];
